A||||6||Formula 18||3.1L Question (kinda long)||||1||
Z||000000||Formula 18||01-04-2001||10:22 PM||||I have been reading thought the threads about 3.1L and nearly all of them say you have to switch the pistons on the 3.1L with AL heads. I know you have to switch heads so that you may switch intakes so you can keep your ECM BUT why do you switch pistons. I thought I read some where that it was b/c of a differance in combustion chambers.<P>What would happen if you took a rotating assembly from a 3.1L with AL heads and swaped te top half to all the fiero stuff. Basically just used the 3.1 as a short block?? Are there clearance problem or just low compression? I am thinking about running a turbo and if there are no clearance problems this might be a good wat to build up boost PSI. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-04-2001||10:41 PM||||I don't remember the exact numbers but I believe the compression worked out to somewhere around 5.5 or 6 to one.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Standard||01-05-2001||04:32 AM||||That was covered in the Fiero mailing list a while ago.. dang it, i deleted those messages.  If I remember right, the compression ratio came out to around 8:1 with the dished pistons and iron heads, maybe a little bit lower.  <BR>Did find a few messages from one guy that kept the dished pistons and had some advice for a turbo.. here's the text:<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>What about a 3.1 with a turbo?<P>I have no experience with turbos, however, if you're serious about one, the<BR>compression ratio you'll end up with will be suitable for a turbo.  The<BR>3.1L pistons ONLY come dished, so if you leave a little deck height on the<BR>block, use the thicker head gasket, and just do a light clean up cut on the<BR>heads, you'll be ready.<P>I went the opposite way and had the heads cc'd to 45.5, zero-deck the<BR>block, and Im using Fel-Pro's thin head gasket.  Doing the math I will have<BR>a 9.51 to 1 compression ratio.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>his email addy is  <A HREF="mailto:rhorton@azstarnet.com">rhorton@azstarnet.com</A> <P><BR>||||reg||
Z||000003||Formula 18||01-05-2001||12:54 PM||||I was looking on KB-silvolite's website and it said that the al head have a dish in the piston that is 26cc and compared the the 12cc dish in the Iron heads...<P>Aluminum Head. Lumina.<BR>2.88" dia. head recess .247" deep.<BR>APPROXIMATE HEAD<BR>VOLUME=26cc<BR>   Comp. Ht. 1.450<BR>   Pin Dia..9054 Offset <P>Iron Head. <BR>2.95" dia. head recess .106" deep. <BR>APPROXIMATE HEAD<BR>VOLUME=12cc<BR> Comp. Ht. 1.450<BR> Pin Dia..9055 Offset<P>Can anyone do the math?? <P>------------------<BR>Brian<BR> -88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula 18||01-07-2001||12:37 AM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000005||ironhedno3||01-27-2001||01:05 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula 18:<BR><B>I have been reading thought the threads about 3.1L and nearly all of them say you have to switch the pistons on the 3.1L with AL heads. I know you have to switch heads so that you may switch intakes so you can keep your ECM BUT why do you switch pistons. I thought I read some where that it was b/c of a differance in combustion chambers.<P>What would happen if you took a rotating assembly from a 3.1L with AL heads and swaped te top half to all the fiero stuff. Basically just used the 3.1 as a short block?? Are there clearance problem or just low compression? I am thinking about running a turbo and if there are no clearance problems this might be a good wat to build up boost PSI. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>try using the ironhed 3.1 from 91-93 lumna apv vans and pontiac transports..all fiero stuff will bolt to it<p>[This message has been edited by ironhedno3 (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000006||GT Bastard||01-27-2001||08:19 PM||||I think I've finally come to the conclusion that NOBODY knows what the compression ratio will be with stock Fiero heads.  I'm gonna buy some Lumina APV heads...  ||||reg||1
